%% CASE Hartwell & Co AI legal platform
Hartwell & Co, an international law firm, is rolling out Lexora, an AI-assisted platform for contract review and legal research. A vendor provides the core platform, the firm's own agile team configures workflows and integrations, and the rollout to 600 lawyers in eight offices follows a phased plan.

- The partners' **management committee** must approve any use of AI on client matters.
- Client confidentiality and legal privilege rules apply, and **some clients prohibit their data from leaving their country**.
- The bar association's guidance says that **lawyers remain responsible for AI-generated work** and must be able to explain it.
- Senior associates fear that AI will reduce the **billable hours** on which their bonuses depend.
- A new **AI law** will require risk assessments and human oversight for "high-risk" AI systems from next year.
@@ BE1 | Predictive
A practice group wants to start using Lexora on a client matter before the management committee has approved the use of AI. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that committee approval is required, and help the group prepare its request
B) Allow it, since the practice group's partners have accepted responsibility for the matter
C) Allow it on one small matter only, as a pilot that the committee does not need to approve
D) Ask the vendor to confirm that its platform is safe enough to use without any approval
= A
? Governance rules define who may approve what. The project manager supports compliance with them, for example by helping the group make its case to the committee.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
Some clients prohibit their data from leaving their country. How should the rollout address this?
A) Identify those matters and keep their data in-country, or exclude them for now
B) Ignore the restriction, since the platform's data centres are secure and well certified
C) Ask the affected clients to agree to the transfer of their data during the next meeting
D) Store the data abroad but encrypt it, which satisfies every data residency requirement
= A
? Contractual and legal data residency requirements must be met. Matters are identified and handled in a compliant way before the platform is used on them.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
Senior associates fear that the AI platform will reduce the billable hours on which their bonuses depend. What should the project do?
A) Raise it with the partners so incentives are reviewed, and show the higher-value work it enables
B) Tell the associates that their concerns are unfounded, since billable hours will not change at all
C) Make the use of the platform compulsory and monitor how many hours each associate bills
D) Ignore the concern, since bonuses are decided by the partners and are not part of the project
= A
? Resistance often reflects real interests. Aligning incentives with the new way of working, and showing its benefits, addresses the cause of the resistance.

@@ BE8 | Agile
A new AI law will require risk assessments and human oversight for high-risk AI systems from next year. What should the team do?
A) Assess whether the law applies, and add the required controls to the backlog in time
B) Wait until the law takes effect next year before deciding whether it applies to the platform
C) Stop using AI in the platform completely, so that the new law cannot apply to the firm
D) Ask the vendor to deal with the law, since the vendor provides the platform's AI models
= A
? External regulatory changes are assessed early. The product owner adds compliance work to the backlog so that it is done before the deadline.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
Who should have the authority to stop offshore operations for safety reasons?
A) Anyone on site, under the stop-work authority set by the safety rules
B) Only the project manager, after consulting the sponsor and the steering committee
C) Only the vessel captain, since the vessel is the captain's responsibility at sea
D) Only the client's representative, since the client owns the wind farm being built
= A
? Stop-work authority for safety is deliberately given to everyone, so that unsafe work can be stopped immediately without waiting for permission.

@@ BE1 | Agile
Developers want to publish part of the product's code as open source. What should they do?
A) Follow the policy and get approval for the licence and intellectual property
B) Publish it, since open-source contributions are always good for the organisation
C) Publish it under their own names, since they wrote the code in their spare time
D) Ask the product owner to decide, since the code belongs to the product
= A
? Releasing code involves intellectual property and licensing decisions, which are governed by organisational policy and approvals.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
Which governance checkpoint must be passed before a new food product is released for sale?
A) A formal release approval confirming that food safety and quality requirements are met
B) A marketing review confirming that the packaging design is attractive to customers
C) A sprint review in which the production team demonstrates the new product
D) A lessons learned session with the team that developed the product's recipe
= A
? Release gates confirm that mandatory requirements, such as food safety, are met before products reach customers.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
A major tournament has many functional areas, such as venues, security, ticketing and transport. Which governance structure fits best?
A) A steering committee with clearly defined functional leads and escalation routes
B) Each functional area working independently, with no central coordination
C) The project manager making every decision for every functional area personally
D) A weekly vote of all staff and volunteers on the main decisions to be made
= A
? Complex events need clear decision rights at each level, coordinated by a steering body with defined escalation routes.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid | multi
A team member is worried about unethical practices on the project but fears retaliation. Which two features should the organisation's whistleblowing channel provide? (Choose two.)
A) Confidentiality for the person raising the concern
B) Protection from retaliation
C) Public naming of the person who reported
D) Mandatory identification of the reporter
E) Immediate dismissal of the person accused
= A, B
? Effective speak-up channels protect reporters through confidentiality and non-retaliation, while concerns are investigated fairly.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
The wind farm's permit requires marine mammal observers and a "soft start" for piling work. What should the project do?
A) Build these measures into the method statements and the schedule, and record compliance
B) Apply the measures only on the days when inspectors are expected to visit the construction site
C) Ask the piling contractor to decide whether the measures are needed on each day
D) Skip the soft start when the schedule is behind, since it slows down the piling work
= A
? Permit conditions are compliance obligations. They are planned into the work methods and schedule, and evidence of compliance is kept.

@@ BE2 | Agile
Marketing wants to show betting adverts in the club's app, which many under-18s use. What should the product owner do?
A) Check the rules with legal, and apply age restrictions or remove the adverts
B) Show the adverts, since betting companies are the club's most important commercial partners
C) Show the adverts only at night, when younger users are less likely to be using the app
D) Let marketing decide, since advertising is outside the product owner's responsibility
= A
? Products must comply with advertising and gambling rules, especially where minors are involved. Legal advice guides the right design.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
A recipe change adds a small amount of nuts to a product. What must the project make sure of?
A) The allergen labelling is updated and verified before the product is released
B) The change is small enough that the existing label can still be used
C) Customers are told about the change through the company's social media
D) The nuts are listed on the label only when the next packaging is printed
= A
? Allergen information is a critical legal and safety requirement. Labels must be correct before any product reaches consumers.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
Event volunteers are asked to work full shifts with set hours, and they receive cash "thank-you" payments. What should the project manager do?
A) Check whether this creates employment obligations, and adjust the arrangement
B) Continue, since volunteers are never covered by employment law, whatever they are paid
C) Increase the cash payments, so that the volunteers are happier with their long shifts
D) Ask the volunteers to sign a statement that they are not employees of the organisation
= A
? The legal status of volunteers depends on the actual arrangement. Paid, controlled work can create employment obligations that must be met.

@@ BE2 | Agile
The fan app will allow users under 13 to create accounts. What should the product owner do?
A) Apply the legal requirements for children's data, such as parental consent, in the design
B) Treat children's accounts like adult accounts, since the app collects little personal data
C) Ask children to confirm that they are over 13 and accept whatever age they enter
D) Allow children's accounts now and add consent features only if regulators ask for them
= A
? Children's personal data is protected by specific legal requirements, such as parental consent, which are built into the product's design.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
New soil data requires changes to some foundation designs, which a certification body has already approved. What should happen?
A) The change goes through change control, including re-approval by the certification body
B) The engineers update the designs and inform the certification body after installation
C) The contractor adapts the foundations on site to suit the soil conditions that it finds when piling
D) The original designs are used, since they have already been approved and certified
= A
? Changes to certified designs are controlled formally, including any external re-approval, before the work is carried out.

@@ BE3 | Agile
Lawyers frequently ask for changes to the platform's standard contract templates. How should these requests be managed?
A) Through the backlog, with legal review and version control of the templates
B) By letting every lawyer edit the templates directly whenever they wish, to save time
C) By refusing all changes, since the templates were approved at launch
D) By collecting the requests and making all changes once a year
= A
? Template changes are prioritised like other work, reviewed by qualified lawyers and version-controlled, so that everyone uses the approved version.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
Procurement wants to switch to a cheaper supplier of a key ingredient. What should happen?
A) It is assessed through change control, including quality, allergen and label impacts
B) Procurement makes the switch, since buying ingredients is its responsibility alone
C) The production team tries the new ingredient on the line and decides itself
D) The switch is made, and the impacts are reviewed after the first production run with the new supplier
= A
? Changes that can affect quality and safety are assessed before approval, with all of the affected areas considered.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
Two months before the tournament, the broadcaster asks for new camera platforms in the stadium. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact on safety, cost and schedule, and decide through change control
B) Build the platforms immediately, since the broadcaster pays for the rights to show the tournament
C) Refuse the request, since the stadium's design was frozen several months ago
D) Ask the construction contractor to fit the platforms in wherever it can find space
= A
? Late changes are analysed for their impacts, especially on safety, and decided formally with the requester.

@@ BE3 | Agile
An A/B test shows that a new ticket checkout reduces sales. What should the product owner do?
A) Switch back to the old version and update the backlog based on what was learned
B) Keep the new version, since a lot of effort went into building it
C) Extend the test to all users to see whether the sales recover over the following months
D) Hide the results, since stakeholders liked the new design in the reviews
= A
? Experiments inform decisions. When a change reduces value, it is rolled back and the learning shapes the next step.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
On the day of an outdoor event, a storm forces the organisers to move an activity indoors. How should this change be handled?
A) Use the agreed contingency plan and emergency decision rights, then document it
B) Wait for the steering committee to meet and approve the change formally before acting
C) Let each staff member decide what to do when the storm reaches the site
D) Continue outdoors as planned, since the change has not been approved
= A
? Emergency changes are handled through pre-agreed plans and decision rights, and are documented afterwards.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
The installation vessel's crane breaks down, and repairs will take ten days. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact, activate contingency options and update the schedule and stakeholders
B) Wait for the repairs to be completed before taking any further action on the installation schedule
C) Terminate the vessel charter immediately and look for a new vessel on the market
D) Ask the crew to continue lifting with the damaged crane to avoid any delay
= A
? Issues are analysed for impact, contingency plans are activated and stakeholders are informed. Unsafe workarounds are never acceptable.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Stories are blocked because nobody can say how a new regulation should be interpreted. What should the scrum master do?
A) Escalate for an authoritative interpretation, while the team continues with other work
B) Let the developers interpret the regulation as they think best, and move on with the stories
C) Remove all of the stories that are affected by the regulation from the backlog
D) Wait until the regulator publishes guidance, however long that takes
= A
? The scrum master pursues a decision from the right authority, while the team keeps delivering value elsewhere.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
Two serious issues need the same two engineers at the same time. What should the project manager do?
A) Prioritise by impact and urgency with stakeholders, and seek more expert support
B) Let the engineers decide for themselves which of the two issues they want to work on
C) Ask the engineers to split their time equally between the two issues every day
D) Work on the issue that was reported first, whatever the impact of the other one
= A
? Scarce resources are directed to the most important issues, based on impact and urgency, while extra capacity is sought.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
The ticketing system fails on the first day of sales. What should the project manager do?
A) Activate the incident plan: fix it with the vendor, inform fans and adjust the sales plan
B) Wait for the vendor to fix the system before informing anyone about the failure
C) Ask fans to try again every hour until the system starts working again, without further information
D) Cancel the ticket sales for the whole tournament to avoid further problems
= A
? Incidents are managed through prepared plans that cover technical recovery, communication and business continuity.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Which measure shows whether impediments are being removed quickly?
A) The average time that impediments stay open
B) The number of team members in each team
C) The number of stories in the product backlog
D) The length of the sprint review meeting
= A
? Tracking how long impediments remain open shows whether the team and organisation are removing obstacles effectively.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
Issues are often closed without checking with the people who raised them, who then raise them again. What should the project manager do?
A) Confirm the resolution with the person who raised each issue before closing it
B) Stop accepting new issues from people who have already raised the same issue before
C) Close issues automatically after two weeks, whatever their status
D) Keep every issue open until the end of the project to be safe
= A
? An issue is resolved only when the person affected agrees that it has been addressed. Confirmation prevents repeated reporting.

@@ BE5 | Predictive | fill
There is a 15% probability that the export cable will be damaged during installation, which would cost $2,000,000. What is the expected monetary value of this risk? (Answer in dollars.)
= $300,000
? EMV = probability × impact = 0.15 × 2,000,000 = $300,000. This can be compared with the cost of responses, such as insurance or extra protection.

@@ BE5 | Agile
The AI tool sometimes cites court cases that do not exist. Which response best addresses this risk?
A) Require verification of every citation against its source, with links to the originals
B) Accept the risk, since the tool is correct most of the time and saves the lawyers a great deal of time
C) Remove the citation feature completely, even though the lawyers find it very valuable
D) Ask the vendor to promise that the tool will never produce incorrect citations again
= A
? Mitigation combines a control (human verification) with design support (links to sources), keeping lawyers accountable for the output.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
How often should the risks of a fast-changing project be reviewed?
A) Frequently, as part of regular project meetings
B) Once, during the planning phase of the project
C) Only at the end of each major project phase
D) Only when a risk has actually occurred
= A
? Risk management is continuous. Frequent reviews keep the register current as the project and its environment change.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
If heavy rain is forecast 48 hours before the event, the opening ceremony will move indoors. What type of risk response is this?
A) A contingent response with a trigger
B) A risk avoidance strategy for the event
C) A transfer of the risk to the venue owner
D) A passive acceptance of the weather risk
= A
? A contingent response is executed only if a defined trigger occurs, here a forecast of heavy rain 48 hours before the event.

@@ BE5 | Agile
The platform depends on a single AI model provider. How can the team reduce this risk?
A) Design the platform so that the AI model can be replaced, and monitor the provider
B) Sign a ten-year contract with the provider so that it cannot stop providing the service
C) Accept the risk, since the provider is a large company that is unlikely to fail
D) Build the firm's own AI model from scratch immediately, whatever the cost and time
= A
? Reducing dependency through a replaceable design, combined with monitoring, mitigates the risk at reasonable cost.

@@ BE5 | Predictive | match
Match each risk analysis technique with its description.
1) Monte Carlo simulation
2) Sensitivity analysis
3) Decision tree analysis
4) Probability and impact matrix
A) Models the combined effect of uncertainties on project outcomes
B) Shows which risks have the greatest effect on an outcome
C) Compares options whose outcomes are uncertain
D) Prioritises individual risks by their probability and impact ratings
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Quantitative techniques analyse the overall effect of uncertainty, while the probability and impact matrix supports qualitative prioritisation of individual risks.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
The team reviews each of the project's assumptions to see which could be wrong and what would happen if they were. What is this technique called?
A) Assumption and constraint analysis
B) Earned value analysis of the plan
C) Critical path analysis of the schedule
D) Resource levelling of the team
= A
? Assumption and constraint analysis turns uncertain assumptions into identified risks that can be analysed and managed.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
After an object was dropped from height offshore, which approach best prevents it from happening again?
A) A structured root cause investigation, with actions and lessons shared widely
B) A reminder to all workers to be more careful when they are working at height
C) Disciplinary action against the worker who dropped the object, as a warning to others
D) An extra inspection at the end of each shift, without changing the work methods
= A
? Root cause investigation finds the system causes behind incidents, so that effective actions prevent recurrence across the project.

@@ BE6 | Agile
The team has many improvement ideas. Which technique can it use to choose quickly which ones to work on first?
A) Dot voting
B) Earned value analysis
C) Critical path analysis
D) A procurement audit
= A
? Dot voting lets the team prioritise many options quickly and visibly, so that improvement effort focuses on the ideas the team values most.

@@ BE6 | Hybrid
The team wants to cut the line's changeover time from 90 minutes to 30 minutes. Which approach fits best?
A) A focused improvement workshop using a quick changeover method such as SMED
B) Buying a second production line so that changeovers are no longer needed at all
C) Asking the operators to work faster during every changeover from now on
D) Reducing the number of products made on the line to avoid changeovers
= A
? Rapid improvement workshops with methods such as SMED (single-minute exchange of die) systematically shorten changeovers.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
Which lean method organises the workplace through sort, set in order, shine, standardise and sustain?
A) 5S
B) PDCA
C) DMAIC
D) RACI
= A
? 5S creates an organised, clean and standardised workplace, which reduces waste and errors and supports continuous improvement.

@@ BE6 | Agile
A Kanban team holds a regular meeting to review its delivery against customer expectations and to improve its service. What is this meeting called?
A) A service delivery review
B) A phase gate review
C) A procurement review
D) A design review
= A
? In Kanban, the service delivery review examines how well the team meets customer expectations, such as lead times, and drives improvement.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
A nearby coal plant will close, and the wind farm's operator could employ some of its workers. How can the project support this?
A) Work with the operator and training providers on retraining programmes
B) Leave it to the coal plant's workers to find training on their own
C) Hire only experienced wind technicians from other countries
D) Promise jobs to all of the coal plant's workers immediately
= A
? Organisational and social change is supported through partnerships and retraining, which also builds local support for the project.

@@ BE7 | Agile
Coaches doubt the value of a new performance analytics tool. What would most help its adoption?
A) Involve a few respected coaches early, show quick wins and let them shape it
B) Requiring all coaches to use the tool for every training session from next week
C) Introducing the tool without discussion, since the coaches will see its value later
D) Asking the club's board to tell the coaches to stop doubting the new tool
= A
? Respected early adopters, quick wins and involvement in shaping the tool build credibility and desire to adopt it.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid | multi
Automation will change the operators' jobs from manual packing to monitoring machines. Which two practices help them through this change? (Choose two.)
A) Involving them in designing the new roles
B) Training them in the new skills needed
C) Announcing the changes on the go-live day
D) Keeping the new roles deliberately vague
E) Removing all support soon after go-live
= A, B
? Involvement and training help people move into redesigned roles with confidence and reduce resistance.

@@ BE7 | Predictive
Who is usually the most effective person to explain to employees how a change will affect their daily work?
A) Their direct line manager
B) An external consultant
C) The vendor's trainer
D) The chief financial officer
= A
? Employees usually trust their direct managers most for information about how a change affects their own work.

@@ BE7 | Agile
How can the change team find out how employees feel during the rollout, so that it can adjust its approach?
A) Short, regular pulse surveys, with visible follow-up actions
B) A single long survey after the rollout has been completed
C) Asking the managers to guess how their staff are feeling
D) Waiting for complaints to reach the human resources department
= A
? Frequent, lightweight feedback lets the change team adapt during the rollout. Acting visibly on the feedback encourages honest responses.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
The grid operator announces that it may curtail output from new wind farms at times of low demand. What should the project manager do?
A) Assess the impact on the business case and consider options such as storage
B) Continue as planned, since the wind farm's grid connection has already been agreed
C) Stop the project until the grid operator withdraws the announcement
D) Ask the turbine manufacturer to compensate the project for lost output
= A
? Changes in market and infrastructure rules can affect value. They are assessed, and options are considered with the sponsor.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
Demand for plant-based products is rising quickly. How should the project for the new production line respond?
A) Assess whether the line should also handle plant-based products
B) Continue with the original design, since the line was approved for the current products
C) Redesign the line for plant-based products only, without any further analysis
D) Wait until competitors have launched plant-based products before reconsidering
= A
? Market trends are assessed for their effect on the project's value, and the design or business case is adapted where justified.

@@ BE8 | Agile
The league signs a deal to stream every match online. How might this affect the club's app?
A) The product owner assesses new opportunities and reprioritises the roadmap
B) It has no effect, since the app was designed before the streaming deal was signed
C) The team stops all other work and builds a streaming service of its own immediately
D) The club asks the league to cancel the deal because it complicates the app's roadmap
= A
? External changes can create opportunities. The product owner evaluates them and adapts the roadmap based on value.

@@ BE8 | Predictive
A new government announces that it will cut renewable energy subsidies for projects that are not yet operating. What should the project manager do?
A) Reassess the business case and options, such as faster commissioning, with the sponsor
B) Continue as planned, since the subsidy cut may never actually be introduced into law
C) Stop the project immediately, since it will no longer be profitable without the subsidies
D) Ask the team to work unpaid overtime so that the farm is operating before the cut
= A
? Political changes can alter a project's value. The business case is reassessed and options are evaluated with the sponsor.
